WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.439
The increasing incidence of hospital-
acquired staphylococcal infection is

00:00:25.439 --> 00:00:29.939
prompting nurses and other medical
personnel to re-evaluate procedures

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.829
employed in patient care. Since most disease producing organisms

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:39.210
are destroyed when proper septic
procedures are used, hand-washing is

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.380
considered the most practical and
consistent defense against the spread of infection.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:50.080
The following scenes demonstrate an accepted method requiring 60 to 90 seconds to perform.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.900
Hand-washing should include the forearms up to the area pointed out by the nurse.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.500
Using either cake or liquid soap, first lather the hands thoroughly,

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:16.560
then rub the dorsal and palmar surfaces applying approximately ten friction movements.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.620
Next, interlace the fingers and cleanse the interdigital spaces, again with ten friction movements.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.660
Now soak the forearms and rub each for
10 to 15 seconds.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:01.580
Always rinse carefully, allowing the
water to flow from forearms toward the fingertips.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.910
After repeating the hand-washing
procedure, clean the fingernails.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:20.200
A toothbrush may be used for this purpose.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:38.460
Dry the hands and forearms thoroughly
with a paper towel.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.660
Then with a dry portion of the towel, turn off the water faucet.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.700
To prevent skin irritation, complete the handwashing with an application of a suitable lotion or cream.

